# A first workspace

A *workspace* is a directory of `.nml` files with an `nml.toml` at its root. NML builds it into a site: one HTML page per page file, linked to one another.

## Creating one

```sh
nml init notes
cd notes
```

`nml init` writes a starting `nml.toml`, and in `.nml/` what editors use: the declarations of the Lua API for [LuaLS](https://luals.github.io), and the sources of the standard modules. Running it again in a workspace only refreshes `.nml/`.

## Writing a page

Every `.nml` file is a page unless [[configuration#pages|the configuration]] says otherwise. Write `index.nml`:

:example
````
# Notes

Some *notes*, with a [link](https://example.org)
and a list:

 * First
 * Second
````

The [[text#]] page starts the tour of the syntax.

## Building

```sh
nml build
```

Pages are written to `public/`: `index.nml` becomes `public/index.html`, `guide/setup.nml` becomes `public/guide/setup.html`. Next to them are `nml.css`, the default style, and the media pages use.

Builds are incremental: only the files that changed are parsed again, and only the pages whose content or links changed are written. Errors are printed with where they are, and make `nml build` fail. See [[building#]].

## Previewing

```sh
nml serve --open
```

`nml serve` serves the workspace at `http://127.0.0.1:<port>`, and opens it in the browser with `--open`. It watches the files: the page in the browser follows each saved change. Nothing is written to `public/`.

Editors do better: with the [[editors#|language server]], the preview follows every change before it is saved, and scrolls to where the cursor is.

## One file

A single file needs no workspace:

```sh
nml build letter.nml
nml build letter.nml --output letter.html
```

Its page is written next to it, unless `--output` says where.
